// Samurai: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== samurai.f01 | feature | journey
title: The Far Side Of The Sea
genre: samurai + historical-drama
tone: Wide, courteous and slowly bereft
era: 1613 | period
setting: A galleon's road from northern Japan to Mexico, Spain and Rome, and home again | real | sea
logline: A provincial samurai is sent across two oceans to ask the Pope and the King of Spain for trade, is baptised to further his lord's errand, and comes home seven years later to a country where his new faith is a capital crime.
premise: A northern lord builds a ship on a foreign pattern and sends an embassy the wrong way round the world. Its leader, {jinnai}, has never seen the sea from a deck. He carries his lord's letter in a lacquer box and understands his duty exactly: come back with a treaty. Nobody has told him that while he travels, the rulers at home are closing the country.
synopsis: The film is a journey out and a journey back, and the two do not match. Out: {jinnai} and a company of samurai and merchants cross the Pacific with a Franciscan, {brother_mateo}, who interprets and has plans of his own, namely to be made bishop of the north. In Mexico the samurai are a marvel; some of the merchants are baptised for the trade's sake. In Spain the king receives them with great politeness and gives no answer. {brother_mateo} explains that a Christian ambassador would be heard differently. {jinnai} considers it as he would a tactical problem. He is baptised in the king's presence. To his surprise something in him is not acting; his retainer {genpachi} watches his lord's man kneel to a foreign god and says nothing, in the manner of a man keeping a list. In Rome there is a procession, a portrait, a citizenship on parchment, and still no treaty: letters have arrived from Japan ahead of them, describing executions of Christians. The courts of Europe become kind in the way of people who have decided. The way back takes four years. Some of the company slip away in a Spanish river town and stay for good. {jinnai} goes on, because the letter in the box has to be answered in person even if the answer is nothing. He lands at home to edicts on every post. His lord, {lord_north}, receives him at night, alone, thanks him for his service and does not ask what is in his baggage. The question of the film's last act is small and total: a rosary, a painted portrait of himself at prayer, a Roman parchment. He is told that a man who quietly gave these up would be left in peace. He reports, in full, to his lord. Then he goes home to his estate, and puts the things in a chest, and does not burn them. He dies within two years, in his bed. The chest is found by officials many years later, and kept.
question: What do you owe a master whose errand changed you?
themes: duty carried past its purpose; conversion as strategy and as accident; a door closing behind a traveller
audience: Adults; lovers of historical epics on a human scale
viewpoint: With {jinnai}; Europe is seen as he sees it, strange in its details and not in its politics
stakes: A treaty, a lord's ambitions, and the conscience of a man who will come home to a different law
conflict: A loyal envoy against distance, time and two courts that will not say no, and then against the edicts of his own country
ending: The embassy fails. He comes home, reports, and keeps what he was told to burn. He dies quietly; the chest survives.
experience: The awe of first sight, a long dying fall, and a final silence with an object in it
look: A black galleon on two oceans; kimono in baroque rooms; snow on a northern estate; a small painted portrait
sound: Ship's timbers, plainchant heard through a foreign ear, a lacquer box opening. Wind in pines at the end.
m: prove | time | love-vs-duty | bittersweet
@ jinnai | Hasegawa Jinnai | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 42 | To bring his lord an answer | His family's standing rides on this errand | The answer is no, and he is no longer the man who left | Grave, curious, incapable of half-measures | From soldier of an errand to keeper of a private faith | Formal, spare; learns Spanish like a drill | Travelling kimono, two swords, a lacquer letter box
@ brother_mateo | Brother Mateo Soler | co-lead | foil | human | he/him | 39 | A bishopric in the north | He truly wants souls and truly wants rank | Rome has read the letters from Japan | Eloquent, tireless, vain | From guide to a man whose scheme has stranded his friend | Florid in three languages | Grey habit, sandals, a satchel of petitions
@ genpachi | Genpachi | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 50 | His master home alive and himself unpolluted | He has served the house thirty years | He cannot follow where his master has gone | Loyal, dry, conservative | From silent disapproval to guarding the chest | Few words; proverbs | Plain hakama, a spear he carries round the world
@ lord_north | The Lord Of The North | supporting | mentor | human | he/him | 53 | Trade, and then deniability | He dreamed of ships | The capital is watching him | Brilliant, far-sighted, practical | From sender of embassies to a man who must not know | Easy authority, indirect | Indoor robe, a fan, a brazier
~ jinnai - brother_mateo | envoy and interpreter | dependence | pity for a man who used him and meant it kindly
~ jinnai - genpachi | master and retainer | unquestioned obedience | a chest hidden by the one who disapproved
~ jinnai - lord_north | vassal and lord | a letter in a box | a report nobody will write down
# Outward | EXT | Shipyard, Northern Bay | DAY | jinnai, lord_north, genpachi, brother_mateo | Be sent | A foreign-pattern ship on the slip. A letter sealed into lacquer. The lord says: come back with their word | The embassy sails | 5 | water, crowd=200, vehicles=galleon, props=letter box
# Outward | EXT | Galleon Deck, Pacific | DAY | jinnai, genpachi, brother_mateo | Cross | Ninety days. {jinnai} learns the names of sails and twelve Spanish verbs. {genpachi} is seasick with dignity | | 4 | water, vehicles=galleon, vfx=open ocean
# The Courts | INT | Royal Palace, Madrid | DAY | jinnai, brother_mateo | Be received | Every courtesy; no answer. The friar explains how a Christian envoy would be heard | {jinnai} decides to be baptised | 6 | crowd=150, props=letter box
# The Courts | INT | Convent Church | DAY | jinnai, genpachi, brother_mateo | Kneel | Water, a new name, a king watching. He finds he is not pretending. In the nave {genpachi} looks at the floor | | 5 | crowd=120
# The Courts | INT | Roman Palace, Antechamber | DAY | jinnai, brother_mateo | Read the faces | A procession yesterday, a portrait, a parchment of citizenship. Today the cardinals are gentle. Letters have come from Japan | The embassy has failed | 6 | crowd=60, props=portrait+parchment
# Homeward | EXT | Spanish River Town, Quay | DAWN | jinnai, genpachi | Count the company | Six of his men do not come to the boat. He does not send for them. He has a box to carry back | | 4 | water, crowd=30
# Homeward | INT | Castle, Private Room | NIGHT | jinnai, lord_north | Report | Edicts on every post between the harbour and here. The lord thanks him and does not ask about his baggage. He is told how a quiet man is left in peace | He is asked to give it up | 6 | props=brazier+letter box
# Homeward | INT | Estate, Storehouse | NIGHT | jinnai, genpachi | Keep it | Snow outside. A rosary, a small portrait, a parchment go into a chest. {genpachi} holds the lamp, and then carries the chest himself | | 4 | props=chest+rosary+portrait
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from a northern domain's embassy to Europe of 1613 to 1620, with invented people
